================================================================
5. EVIDENCE STATUS MODEL
========================

Standardize:

PASS
PASS_WITH_LIMITATIONS
FAIL
UNKNOWN
NOT_APPLICABLE

Evidence levels:

E0 — code exists
E1 — unit tested
E2 — integration tested
E3 — system/multi-process tested
E4 — real external system tested
E5 — production observed

A capability may be:

PASS at E2

without being:

production proven.

Keep status and evidence tier separate.

================================================================
6. EVIDENCE ARTIFACTS
=====================

Every important release gate should record:

gate
status
evidence_level
command
test
environment
timestamp
artifact/result
limitations

Example:

RLS:
status: PASS
evidence: E3
command: pytest tests/integration/test_rls.py
database: PostgreSQL 16
limitations: []

No naked booleans.

================================================================
P0 — REAL POSTGRESQL MULTI-TENANCY PROOF
========================================

Upgrade-10 must not claim hard tenant isolation from SQLite or mocked DB
behavior.

Provision a real disposable PostgreSQL instance.

Use:

CI service container
Docker Compose
Testcontainers
or equivalent.

================================================================
7. VERIFY ACTUAL RLS EXISTENCE
==============================

Inspect enterprise_postgres_store.py and migrations.

Confirm the database actually contains:

ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY

and, where intended:

FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY

plus actual policies.

Do not infer RLS merely from:

SET LOCAL app.current_org

The session variable alone is not RLS.

================================================================
8. DATABASE ROLE MATRIX
=======================

Create explicit roles:

migration/admin role
application role
backup role
read-only operations role where justified

Verify normal application role:

is not superuser
does not have BYPASSRLS
does not own tenant tables in a way that unintentionally bypasses RLS

Test this using PostgreSQL catalog queries.

================================================================
9. RLS OWNER-BYPASS TEST
========================

Mandatory negative test:

normal application connection

attempts cross-tenant SELECT
UPDATE
DELETE
INSERT

while using the actual production DB role.

Prove failure/isolation.

Also test the expected behavior of the table owner and migration role.

Do not hide privileged behavior.

================================================================
10. TENANT CONNECTION CONTEXT
=============================

If using:

SET LOCAL app.current_org = ...

verify it always occurs inside an explicit transaction.

Test:

transaction commit
rollback
exception
pool return
pool reacquire

The next tenant must receive clean context.

================================================================
11. POOL CONTAMINATION TEST
===========================

Use a real connection pool.

Force reuse of the same physical PostgreSQL connection.

Sequence:

Org A transaction
→ return connection

Org B
→ acquire same physical connection

Prove:

Org B cannot observe Org A scope.

Also test after:

exception
cancelled transaction
statement timeout

================================================================
12. MISSING TENANT CONTEXT
==========================

Fail closed.

A request with no valid organization context must never translate to:

all organizations.

Test:

unset variable
empty variable
malformed ID
unknown organization

================================================================
13. CROSS-TENANT TABLE MATRIX
=============================

Test real PostgreSQL isolation for every tenant-owned table.

At minimum:

organizations
projects
memberships
repositories
jobs
events
approvals
artifacts
usage ledger
quota reservations
audit records
API keys/service accounts
GitHub mappings
SCIM resources
policy assignments
outbox where tenant-scoped

For each resource perform:

read
insert
update
delete

from wrong tenant.

================================================================
14. BACKGROUND WORKER TENANT ISOLATION
======================================

HTTP request isolation is insufficient.

Test workers processing:

job
outbox
GitHub webhook
usage event
audit export
SCIM action

Ensure trusted tenant scope comes from authoritative stored mapping.

Never trust a tenant ID supplied inside untrusted task payload.

================================================================
15. REALTIME TENANT ISOLATION
=============================

Test SSE/WebSocket/event subscriptions.

Org A must not subscribe to:

Org B job
Org B events
Org B approvals
Org B usage

including reconnect scenarios.

================================================================
16. ARTIFACT ISOLATION
======================

Test:

guessing artifact ID
replaying signed URL
cross-project path
cross-org artifact download

Possession of ID is never authorization.

================================================================
17. CACHE ISOLATION
===================

Enumerate caches.

Tenant-specific cache keys require tenant scope.

Create poisoning test:

Org A caches resource X.

Org B requests same logical identifier.

Prove no cross-tenant hit.

================================================================
18. API KEY ISOLATION
=====================

Use real auth/service path.

Test:

Org A key -> Org B
Project A key -> Project B
revoked key
expired key
scope missing

Never rely solely on API handler-level tests if store layer can bypass.

================================================================
19. SCIM ISOLATION
==================

Use Organization A SCIM credential.

Attempt:

create user in B
modify B group
deactivate B principal

Must fail.

Test repeated provisioning/idempotency.

================================================================
20. QUOTA RACE WITH REAL POSTGRESQL
===================================

Run multiple processes concurrently against a hard quota.

Prove atomic reservation.

Report:

limit
concurrent requests
accepted
rejected
settled usage
overshoot

Do not claim race safety from single-process locks.

================================================================
21. USAGE LEDGER DEDUPLICATION
==============================

Replay:

same provider usage
same runtime event
same webhook
reconnect sequence

Usage must count once.

Use DB unique/deduplication constraints where appropriate.

================================================================
22. AUDIT ISOLATION
===================

SecurityAuditor for A:

cannot query B.

Platform global administrator behavior must be explicit and audited.

================================================================
23. RLS MIGRATION TEST
======================

Start from previous schema.

Apply migrations.

Verify:

policies
roles
ownership
constraints
indexes

Then execute cross-tenant negative tests.

================================================================
24. BACKUP / RESTORE TENANT TEST
================================

Create at least:

Organization A
Organization B

with:

projects
members
jobs
usage
audit
policies

Backup.

Restore into fresh PostgreSQL.

Re-run cross-tenant tests.

A restored database missing RLS policies is a release blocker.

================================================================
P1 — MULTI-REGION ARCHITECTURE
==============================

Do NOT jump directly to active-active.

First define region semantics precisely.

================================================================
25. REGION DOMAIN MODEL
=======================

Do not use one ambiguous `region`.

Distinguish:

control_plane_region
database_region
worker_region
artifact_region
backup_region
provider_inference_region
GitHub external region/not-controlled
tenant_allowed_regions

================================================================
26. ORGANIZATION RESIDENCY POLICY
=================================

Create an explicit policy model.

Example:

OrganizationResidencyPolicy:
home_region
allowed_worker_regions
allowed_artifact_regions
allowed_backup_regions
allowed_provider_inference_regions
cross_region_transfer_allowed

Do not claim compliance merely from inference_geo.

================================================================
27. RESIDENCY POLICY ENFORCEMENT
================================

Before scheduling:

job requirements
+
organization residency policy
+
worker region
+
runtime capabilities

→ ALLOW/DENY

Record decision.

Do not schedule first and detect residency conflict later.

================================================================
28. WORKER REGION ATTESTATION BOUNDARY
======================================

Do not blindly trust:

worker_region="eu"

from a worker process.

Production architecture needs authoritative worker configuration or
attestation boundary.

For this upgrade, define and enforce trusted deployment metadata.

Do not claim cryptographic attestation unless implemented.

================================================================
29. ARTIFACT REGION
===================

Artifact storage must respect allowed region.

Record:

storage region
object location
organization
retention policy

Never silently copy restricted artifacts cross-region.

================================================================
30. BACKUP REGION
=================

Backup replication is data movement.

Residency policy must include backup locations.

Do not assume DR backup exception automatically satisfies customer
policy.

================================================================
31. PROVIDER INFERENCE REGION
=============================

Keep provider inference geography separate from ZCoder infrastructure
region.

Example:

worker = eu-west
database = eu-central
Anthropic inference_geo = us

This may violate policy even though ZCoder infrastructure itself is EU.

Policy must evaluate the full path.

================================================================
32. DATA FLOW MAP
=================

Create:

docs/DATA-FLOW.md

For every major path document:

source
destination
data type
tenant scope
region
encryption
retention

Paths include:

GitHub -> ZCoder
ZCoder -> Anthropic
worker -> PostgreSQL
worker -> artifact storage
telemetry
backup
SCIM
audit export

================================================================
33. SINGLE-REGION AUTHORITY
===========================

Define current authoritative region.

If PostgreSQL has one writable primary:

state clearly:

CONTROL PLANE = SINGLE-WRITER

Do not market this as active-active.

================================================================
34. REGIONAL WORKERS
====================

Allow multiple worker regions if supported.

Scheduler must consider:

latency
residency
runtime
trust
capacity

A remote worker must still coordinate through authoritative control-plane
state safely.

================================================================
35. CROSS-REGION LATENCY
========================

Test or simulate realistic database latency.

Observe:

lease renewal
claims
scheduler latency
outbox
worker heartbeat

Do not configure lease timings that only work on localhost.

================================================================
36. CLOCK SKEW
==============

Continue using database/server time for lease authority where possible.

Test workers with intentionally skewed local clocks.

Local wall clock must not override DB lease authority.

================================================================
37. REGION FAILURE MODEL
========================

Document expected behavior if:

worker region lost
API region lost
database region lost
artifact region unavailable
Anthropic geography unavailable

Do not claim automatic recovery where not implemented.

================================================================
38. CONTROL PLANE FAILOVER BOUNDARY
===================================

If PostgreSQL HA is external:

explicitly say:

database failover is infrastructure responsibility.

ZCoder should still:

reconnect
reconcile
avoid duplicate side effects

after endpoint recovery.

================================================================
39. DATABASE FAILOVER TEST
==========================

If practical, test using:

primary endpoint interruption
or controlled PostgreSQL failover simulation.

Verify:

connection pool refresh
transactions rollback
leases reconcile
outbox remains idempotent

Do not call it HA failover if only the process restarted on the same DB.

================================================================
40. NETWORK PARTITION MODEL
===========================

Document behavior:

worker can reach Anthropic but not PostgreSQL
worker can reach PostgreSQL but not GitHub
API cannot reach DB
regional network partially fails

Fencing means a disconnected worker must not continue mutating external
systems indefinitely after losing authoritative lease renewal.

================================================================
41. LEASE LOSS BEHAVIOR
=======================

Define:

lease renewal failure threshold

After losing authoritative ownership:

stop/pause new external mutations.

Do not continue for an unbounded grace period.

================================================================
42. MULTI-REGION JOB RECOVERY
=============================

For a lost regional worker:

reconstructible Git workspace?
Managed Agent remote session?
local process state?

Define per runtime.

Do not claim universal transparent failover.

================================================================
P1 — ENCRYPTION BOUNDARY
========================

Build a clean encryption architecture without pretending to implement
full BYOK unless actually done.

================================================================
43. ENCRYPTION CLASSIFICATION
=============================

Classify data:

PUBLIC
INTERNAL
CONFIDENTIAL
SECRET

Examples:

API key hash != plaintext secret
artifact source code = potentially confidential
audit metadata = internal/confidential
private key = secret

================================================================
44. SECRET ENCRYPTION
=====================

Secrets must remain outside ordinary tenant tables where possible.

Use SecretRef.

If encrypted secret storage is introduced:

envelope encryption architecture is preferred.

================================================================
45. ENVELOPE ENCRYPTION MODEL
=============================

Concept:

plaintext data
→ data encryption key
→ ciphertext

DEK
→ wrapped by KEK/KMS

Store:

ciphertext
wrapped DEK
key identifier
algorithm/version

Never store plaintext KEK beside ciphertext.

================================================================
46. KEY PROVIDER INTERFACE
==========================

Create provider-neutral boundary:

KeyProvider:
encrypt/wrap
decrypt/unwrap
rotate

Potential implementations later:

AWS KMS
GCP KMS
Azure Key Vault
Vault/HSM

Do not require a cloud vendor for local mode.

================================================================
47. TENANT KEY BOUNDARY
=======================

Prepare for:

platform key
organization key
resource data key

Do not claim per-tenant customer-managed encryption keys unless
organization-specific key control genuinely exists.

================================================================
48. KEY ROTATION
================

Design:

new writes use new key version
old data remains readable
background rewrap/re-encryption possible
audit rotation

No destructive big-bang rotation.

================================================================
49. CRYPTO METADATA
===================

Persist:

algorithm
key ID
version
created_at

Do not invent custom cryptography.

Use maintained libraries and established AEAD algorithms.

================================================================
50. KEY DELETION CONSEQUENCES
=============================

Treat key destruction as destructive.

Require elevated policy/approval.

Document irrecoverability.

================================================================
51. BACKUP ENCRYPTION RELATIONSHIP
==================================

Database/storage encryption and backup encryption are separate concerns.

Document both.

Do not claim tenant key isolation if backups bypass that boundary.

================================================================
P1 — RETENTION AND LEGAL-HOLD ARCHITECTURE
==========================================

================================================================
52. RETENTION POLICY MODEL
==========================

Define independent retention categories:

jobs
events
artifacts
agent transcripts
audit
usage
identity provisioning history
security events

One global retention duration is insufficient.

================================================================
53. RETENTION RESOLUTION
========================

Policy precedence may be:

platform minimum
organization setting
project setting
resource classification

Lower-level policy must not violate immutable platform/security minimums.

================================================================
54. RETENTION EXECUTOR
======================

Deletion must be:

batched
tenant-scoped
audited
idempotent

Never run unbounded DELETE across all tenants.

================================================================
55. LEGAL HOLD BOUNDARY
=======================

Introduce architectural state such as:

NORMAL
LEGAL_HOLD

only if useful.

Do not claim legal-hold compliance merely because a boolean exists.

At minimum demonstrate:

retention executor refuses protected resource deletion.

================================================================
56. TENANT DELETION
===================

Implement lifecycle:

REQUESTED
SUSPENDED
RETENTION_WAIT
DELETING
VERIFYING
DELETED

Do not hard-delete synchronously from an API request.

================================================================
57. DELETE VERIFICATION
=======================

Verify removal across:

primary DB
artifacts
caches
search/index if any
background queue

Backups may follow separate retention lifecycle.

Document this honestly.

================================================================
P1 — COMPLIANCE EVIDENCE PLATFORM
=================================

This is NOT certification.

Do not claim:

SOC 2 compliant
ISO 27001 certified
HIPAA compliant
PCI compliant

unless an external/qualified compliance process exists.

